To ensure backward compatibility with legacy BGP speakers that only support 2-byte ASNs, configure the `disable-4byte-asn` command on the relevant peers or groups. Apply this at the group or neighbor level to restrict 4-byte ASN negotiation only for legacy peers:

**Group-level configuration:**
```cli
configure router bgp
    group "LEGACY_PEERS"
        disable-4byte-asn
    exit
exit
```

**Neighbor-level configuration:**
```cli
configure router bgp
    group "LEGACY_PEERS"
        neighbor 192.0.2.1
            disable-4byte-asn
        exit
    exit
exit
```

**Key details:**  
- This command prevents 4-byte ASN capability negotiation during BGP session establishment.  
- Apply under `group` or `neighbor` hierarchies targeting legacy peers only.  
- Existing sessions require a reset (e.g., `clear router bgp peer <ip>`) to apply changes.  
- Default behavior (`no disable-4byte-asn`) enables 4-byte ASN support.  

> **Note:** Use per-peer or per-group application to maintain 4-byte ASN support for modern peers. Avoid global BGP-level configuration unless all peers require 2-byte ASN compatibility.
