## Proposed answer:
The recommended approach to drop routes containing your local AS in the AS_PATH while maintaining BGP session stability is to configure an import policy with an AS_PATH filter. Here are the CLI commands:

```cli
/configure router policy-options
begin
as-path "RejectLocalAS"
    expression ".*<your-local-as>.*"
exit
policy-statement "DROP_LOCAL_AS"
    entry 10
        from as-path "RejectLocalAS"
        action reject
    exit
exit
commit
exit

/configure router bgp group "PEER_GROUP" neighbor <neighbor-ip> 
import "DROP_LOCAL_AS"
exit all
```

Replace:
- `<your-local-as>` with your actual local AS number (e.g., 65000)
- `"PEER_GROUP"` with your BGP group name
- `<neighbor-ip>` with the neighbor's IP address

This configuration:
1. Creates an AS_PATH regular expression matching any path containing your local AS
2. Defines a policy to reject routes matching this AS_PATH filter
3. Applies the policy as an import filter to the specific neighbor
4. Maintains session stability while silently discarding problematic routes

The router will now automatically drop any received routes containing your local AS in the AS_PATH attribute.
